om2
note SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-only
note bench.om: the Omega coding benchmark of Omega LLM. One prompt per behaviour, graded by
note compiling the model's function body and running hidden cases, with no help from the harness:
note one sample at temperature 0, no example filter, and the model has to end its own function.
note
note   bench table   TASKS OMC WORK               print the expectation table of the references
note   bench check   TASKS EXPECT OMC WORK        the table equals EXPECT and no two tasks agree
note   bench prompts TASKS WORK                   write WORK/prompt-ID
note   bench sample  TASKS WORK OLLM CMD CK [MERGES]   WORK/answer-ID = OLLM CMD CK 42 128 0 PROMPT [MERGES]
note   bench score   TASKS EXPECT OMC WORK [CORPUS]    grade WORK/answer-ID
note   bench seen    TASKS CORPUS                 tasks whose reference occurs in CORPUS
note
note TASKS (tasks.om) is Omega source. Its `note in1 X`, `note in2 X Y`, `note in3 X Y Z`, `note arr
note v0 .. v7` and `note cnt N` lines are the shared inputs; every task is `note task ID FAMILY`,
note `note say INSTRUCTION` and a reference function `fn tNNN PARAMS` .. `end`. PARAMS is one of
note five shapes: 1 `a ulong to ulong`, 2 `a ulong b ulong to ulong`, 3 `a ulong b ulong c ulong to
note ulong`, 4 `a view ulong 8 n ulong to ulong`, 5 `a edit ulong 8 n ulong to void`. Shapes 1 .. 3
note run on every in1/in2/in3 line, shapes 4 and 5 on every arr with every cnt; shape 5's result is
note the array afterwards. A row of the table is `ID v v ..`, one row per task in file order.
note The prompt is `note INSTRUCTION`, newline, `fn solve PARAMS`, newline; the answer is what the
note model writes after it. Its body runs to the `end` that closes the function (if, while and for
note open a block). Verdicts: pass (the answer is exactly that body and every case holds), passcut
note (the body passes but text follows it: the model did not stop), wrong (a case fails, a trap, or
note a wrong exit), nocompile, noend (no closing end), refused (the body says exit or trap, which
note could fake the result), missing (no answer file), timeout. The headline counts pass only.
note Lines: `R ID FAMILY VERDICT`, then per family `F FAMILY tasks pass passcut wrong nocompile noend
note refused missing timeout seen`, then `SCORE tasks N pass P passcut C seen K`. A task whose
note reference occurs in CORPUS (same text after renaming its names, `bench seen`) is counted as seen
note and left out of N, P and C: it measures recall, not coding.
note Candidate programs run under execpure (OMEGESE section 10: Landlock, seccomp, no new files or
note processes, CPU and memory ceilings); 127 from it means the sandbox is not available and ends
note the run with exit 70 instead of a verdict. Compiles have a 60 s deadline, runs 10 s.
note Exits: 0 done (check: PASS), 1 check failed, 2 usage, 64 a path or packet too long, 65 TASKS
note malformed (stderr names the byte), 66 EXPECT does not fit TASKS, 70 I/O or sandbox, 71 a
note sample command failed. Paths are at most 4096 bytes (Linux PATH_MAX); every table grows with
note its input.

text lin1 "note in1 "
text lin2 "note in2 "
text lin3 "note in3 "
